1
00:00:00,002 --> 00:00:01,222
If you're building an app,

2
00:00:01,222 --> 00:00:02,963
there's one very simple question you need to

3
00:00:02,963 --> 00:00:04,862
ask yourself before anything else.

4
00:00:04,862 --> 00:00:06,353
Who is this app for,

5
00:00:06,353 --> 00:00:08,752
and what do they actually need it for?

6
00:00:08,752 --> 00:00:11,983
That question is the heart of UX research and

7
00:00:11,983 --> 00:00:13,203
is a big part of what I study in

8
00:00:13,203 --> 00:00:15,502
my master's degree in human-computer

9
00:00:15,502 --> 00:00:16,582
interaction. In this video,

10
00:00:16,582 --> 00:00:17,783
I'm going to start from the scratch,

11
00:00:17,783 --> 00:00:19,082
from the beginning,

12
00:00:19,082 --> 00:00:20,002
UI, UX,

13
00:00:20,002 --> 00:00:21,523
and then we'll walk it all the way up

14
00:00:21,523 --> 00:00:22,643
to UX research,

15
00:00:22,643 --> 00:00:23,653
how it is done,

16
00:00:23,653 --> 00:00:25,082
where UX research is,

17
00:00:25,082 --> 00:00:26,043
and all of that.

18
00:00:26,043 --> 00:00:27,722
You've probably heard people

19
00:00:27,722 --> 00:00:29,002
say UI/UX,

20
00:00:29,002 --> 00:00:29,942
like one word,

21
00:00:29,942 --> 00:00:31,002
UI/UX designer,

22
00:00:31,002 --> 00:00:32,143
UI/UX course.

23
00:00:32,143 --> 00:00:35,143
But UI and UX are two different things.

24
00:00:35,143 --> 00:00:36,923
UI stands for user interface.

25
00:00:36,923 --> 00:00:38,343
It is what you can see and touch,

26
00:00:38,343 --> 00:00:38,963
the button,

27
00:00:38,963 --> 00:00:39,602
the colours,

28
00:00:39,602 --> 00:00:40,162
the layout,

29
00:00:40,162 --> 00:00:40,862
the icons,

30
00:00:40,862 --> 00:00:42,102
the screen.

31
00:00:42,102 --> 00:00:43,123
UX, on the other hand,

32
00:00:43,123 --> 00:00:44,623
stands for user experience.

33
00:00:44,623 --> 00:00:46,222
It is how the whole product

34
00:00:46,222 --> 00:00:47,442
feels to use.

35
00:00:47,442 --> 00:00:49,472
Can a user find what they're looking for?

36
00:00:49,472 --> 00:00:51,102
Does it make sense to the user?

37
00:00:51,102 --> 00:00:53,343
Did users get frustrated using it?

38
00:00:53,343 --> 00:00:55,403
Did the user come back to use it?

39
00:00:55,403 --> 00:00:58,023
So you can have a beautiful app with a

40
00:00:58,023 --> 00:00:59,183
beautiful interface

41
00:00:59,183 --> 00:01:01,162
that is a nightmare to use.

42
00:01:01,162 --> 00:01:01,563
In that case,

43
00:01:01,563 --> 00:01:02,862
you have a great UI,

44
00:01:02,862 --> 00:01:04,272
but terrible UX.

45
00:01:04,272 --> 00:01:06,162
So your UI is kind of part of your

46
00:01:06,162 --> 00:01:07,983
UX. The experience is

47
00:01:07,983 --> 00:01:10,632
just much bigger than what is on the screen.

48
00:01:10,632 --> 00:01:12,242
Now, what is UX?

49
00:01:12,242 --> 00:01:14,862
User experience or UX covers

50
00:01:14,862 --> 00:01:17,302
everything about how a person experiences a

51
00:01:17,302 --> 00:01:18,572
product or a service.

52
00:01:18,572 --> 00:01:20,916
The term was popularised by Don Norman when he

53
00:01:20,916 --> 00:01:22,116
was at Apple.

54
00:01:22,116 --> 00:01:26,016
And the way he and Jakob Nielsen describe it

55
00:01:26,016 --> 00:01:28,756
is that user experience covers every part of a

56
00:01:28,756 --> 00:01:30,856
person's interaction with a company,

57
00:01:30,856 --> 00:01:32,776
its services and its products.

58
00:01:32,776 --> 00:01:34,735
And all of this connects back to an idea

59
00:01:34,735 --> 00:01:36,096
called human-centred design.

60
00:01:36,096 --> 00:01:37,375
The idea is simple.

61
00:01:37,375 --> 00:01:39,033
You can make sense of it in...

62
00:01:39,033 --> 00:01:40,399
I love this phrase that I found on,

63
00:01:40,399 --> 00:01:41,222
like, a Medium article.

64
00:01:41,222 --> 00:01:43,343
It says that good design doesn't start with

65
00:01:43,343 --> 00:01:45,642
screens, it starts with people.

66
00:01:45,642 --> 00:01:47,623
So you start from the people who use a

67
00:01:47,623 --> 00:01:48,163
product,

68
00:01:48,163 --> 00:01:48,843
their needs,

69
00:01:48,843 --> 00:01:50,063
their abilities,

70
00:01:50,063 --> 00:01:50,882
their limitations,

71
00:01:50,882 --> 00:01:52,023
their real lives,

72
00:01:52,023 --> 00:01:53,783
and you design around them.

73
00:01:53,783 --> 00:01:54,683
So now,

74
00:01:54,683 --> 00:01:56,243
we know what UX is.

75
00:01:56,243 --> 00:01:57,913
Now, what is UX research?

76
00:01:57,913 --> 00:01:59,963
UX research is how you get to know your

77
00:01:59,963 --> 00:02:02,183
users. It is the work of understanding the

78
00:02:02,183 --> 00:02:03,942
people who would use a product,

79
00:02:03,942 --> 00:02:04,863
what they need,

80
00:02:04,863 --> 00:02:05,702
where they struggle,

81
00:02:05,702 --> 00:02:06,822
what they prioritise,

82
00:02:06,822 --> 00:02:08,303
so that you can build the product the right

83
00:02:08,303 --> 00:02:09,962
way and build it in a way that actually

84
00:02:09,962 --> 00:02:10,803
works

85
00:02:10,803 --> 00:02:11,723
for them.

86
00:02:11,723 --> 00:02:14,512
Why are we doing all this research?

87
00:02:14,512 --> 00:02:15,822
The idea is simple.

88
00:02:15,822 --> 00:02:17,452
You don't want to build the wrong thing.

89
00:02:17,452 --> 00:02:18,783
You could choose to not do it and guess

90
00:02:18,783 --> 00:02:19,763
your way through,

91
00:02:19,763 --> 00:02:21,123
but you don't want to build the wrong thing.

92
00:02:21,123 --> 00:02:22,563
Building is very expensive.

93
00:02:22,563 --> 00:02:23,162
Even with AI,

94
00:02:23,162 --> 00:02:25,003
you still spend your time and your tokens.

95
00:02:25,003 --> 00:02:25,563
And in some cases,

96
00:02:25,563 --> 00:02:27,183
you probably still need a team.

97
00:02:27,183 --> 00:02:29,722
A lot of products fail because the team built

98
00:02:29,722 --> 00:02:30,633
something that

99
00:02:30,633 --> 00:02:32,323
maybe nobody actually needs.

100
00:02:32,323 --> 00:02:34,543
Some other products fail because they build

101
00:02:34,543 --> 00:02:35,282
the right thing,

102
00:02:35,282 --> 00:02:36,842
but they build it in a way that people

103
00:02:36,842 --> 00:02:38,402
just couldn't find their way around it,

104
00:02:38,402 --> 00:02:40,003
or people just couldn't figure it out.

105
00:02:40,003 --> 00:02:42,883
UX research helps to catch both problems early

106
00:02:42,883 --> 00:02:44,883
on. It is cheaper to change your idea or

107
00:02:44,883 --> 00:02:47,070
change your sketch than it is to rebuild an

108
00:02:47,070 --> 00:02:48,563
app that is already live.

109
00:02:48,563 --> 00:02:50,814
And doing UX research means that decisions

110
00:02:50,814 --> 00:02:53,963
about a product come from what the users,

111
00:02:53,963 --> 00:02:54,823
the real people,

112
00:02:54,823 --> 00:02:56,003
actually do.

113
00:02:56,003 --> 00:02:57,383
Otherwise, they just come from a random person

114
00:02:57,383 --> 00:02:58,265
on the team.

115
00:02:58,265 --> 00:02:59,976
Now, who is a UX researcher?

116
00:02:59,976 --> 00:03:00,455
Well, I mean,

117
00:03:00,455 --> 00:03:02,515
a UX researcher does UX research.

118
00:03:02,515 --> 00:03:05,035
A UX researcher works closely with designers,

119
00:03:05,035 --> 00:03:06,196
product managers,

120
00:03:06,196 --> 00:03:07,616
engineers, and the people running the

121
00:03:07,616 --> 00:03:10,395
business. Their job is to bring real users

122
00:03:10,395 --> 00:03:11,256
into

123
00:03:11,256 --> 00:03:12,616
those conversations.

124
00:03:12,616 --> 00:03:14,455
A typical week for a UX researcher might look

125
00:03:14,455 --> 00:03:15,635
like planning a study,

126
00:03:15,635 --> 00:03:17,135
recruiting people to take part,

127
00:03:17,135 --> 00:03:19,816
running user interviews or usability testing,

128
00:03:19,816 --> 00:03:22,095
then sitting down with the notes or the

129
00:03:22,095 --> 00:03:23,785
recordings to find patterns.

130
00:03:23,785 --> 00:03:24,515
And finally,

131
00:03:24,515 --> 00:03:25,376
sharing what they found,

132
00:03:25,376 --> 00:03:27,835
which is found from these users or about these

133
00:03:27,835 --> 00:03:30,595
users, with the team in a way that actually

134
00:03:30,595 --> 00:03:32,196
changes what gets built.

135
00:03:32,196 --> 00:03:34,356
People usually mix up who a UX researcher is

136
00:03:34,356 --> 00:03:36,775
and who a UX designer is a lot.

137
00:03:36,775 --> 00:03:39,186
A UX researcher's job is to understand the

138
00:03:39,186 --> 00:03:40,635
users, what they need.

139
00:03:40,635 --> 00:03:42,775
A UX designer's job is to take that

140
00:03:42,775 --> 00:03:44,496
understanding and design the product around

141
00:03:44,496 --> 00:03:46,155
it. In smaller teams,

142
00:03:46,155 --> 00:03:48,696
there's usually just one person that ends up

143
00:03:48,696 --> 00:03:49,676
doing both jobs.

144
00:03:49,676 --> 00:03:51,135
And there are also a lot of product managers

145
00:03:51,135 --> 00:03:53,015
that do research as part of their jobs as

146
00:03:53,015 --> 00:03:56,575
well. What you need to dive into UX research

147
00:03:56,575 --> 00:03:58,856
is just genuine curiosity about users,

148
00:03:58,856 --> 00:03:59,835
about people.

149
00:03:59,835 --> 00:04:01,395
You have to be really good at taking the

150
00:04:01,395 --> 00:04:02,455
time to study people,

151
00:04:02,455 --> 00:04:03,376
find patterns,

152
00:04:03,376 --> 00:04:05,335
and just be really good at listening.

153
00:04:05,335 --> 00:04:07,075
Basically, you must be interested in people.

154
00:04:07,075 --> 00:04:09,056
When I started reading and studying about UX

155
00:04:09,056 --> 00:04:10,676
research, I kept running into different ways

156
00:04:10,676 --> 00:04:12,265
that people categorise it.

157
00:04:12,265 --> 00:04:13,095
You go on Coursera.

158
00:04:13,095 --> 00:04:14,676
Coursera has quite a number.

159
00:04:14,676 --> 00:04:15,535
You go to different places.

160
00:04:15,535 --> 00:04:16,655
It's

161
00:04:16,655 --> 00:04:18,436
a different kind of categorization everywhere.

162
00:04:18,436 --> 00:04:20,880
You see generative and evaluative,

163
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:22,680
qualitative and quantitative,

164
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:25,080
behavioural and attitudinal primary and

165
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:25,880
secondary, remote,

166
00:04:25,880 --> 00:04:26,600
in-person,

167
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:28,090
moderated, unmoderated.

168
00:04:28,090 --> 00:04:29,780
But the useful way to think about it is

169
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:32,580
that some of these pairs describe what kind of

170
00:04:32,580 --> 00:04:33,400
information you get,

171
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:34,980
like quantitative and qualitative.

172
00:04:34,980 --> 00:04:38,020
And others describe how you run the research,

173
00:04:38,020 --> 00:04:39,740
like remote or in-person.

174
00:04:39,740 --> 00:04:41,200
Anyways, there's a lot of categorization.

175
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:43,390
I will focus on the first three big pairs

176
00:04:43,390 --> 00:04:44,340
in this video,

177
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:45,880
generative and evaluative,

178
00:04:45,880 --> 00:04:47,020
qualitative and quantitative,

179
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:48,300
behavioural and attitudinal.

180
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:50,880
The first big way to split UX research is

181
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:53,220
generative versus evaluative.

182
00:04:53,220 --> 00:04:55,760
Generative UX research happens early before

183
00:04:55,760 --> 00:04:56,880
anything is designed.

184
00:04:56,880 --> 00:04:58,180
It asks questions like,

185
00:04:58,180 --> 00:04:59,300
what is the real problem?

186
00:04:59,300 --> 00:05:00,440
What do people need?

187
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:02,119
How do they get by right now without this

188
00:05:02,119 --> 00:05:03,559
thing that we are about to build?

189
00:05:03,559 --> 00:05:06,320
It generates ideas for what to build.

190
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:06,880
For example,

191
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:07,480
for my thesis,

192
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:08,720
I'm doing generative

193
00:05:08,720 --> 00:05:09,410
research.

194
00:05:09,410 --> 00:05:10,260
Evaluative research,

195
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:11,180
on the other hand,

196
00:05:11,180 --> 00:05:13,460
happens later after there is a design.

197
00:05:13,460 --> 00:05:15,420
It asks questions like,

198
00:05:15,420 --> 00:05:17,080
does this product actually work?

199
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:18,450
Can users use it?

200
00:05:18,450 --> 00:05:20,900
Where do the users get stuck while using it?

201
00:05:20,900 --> 00:05:23,200
So you're evaluating something that already

202
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:25,960
exists. So generative UX research helps you

203
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:27,300
decide what to build.

204
00:05:27,300 --> 00:05:30,220
And evaluative UX research tells you whether

205
00:05:30,220 --> 00:05:31,480
what you build

206
00:05:31,480 --> 00:05:34,940
works. The second way to split UX research is

207
00:05:34,940 --> 00:05:36,760
qualitative versus quantitative.

208
00:05:36,760 --> 00:05:37,480
Quantitative

209
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:39,530
UX research is about numbers.

210
00:05:39,530 --> 00:05:41,240
How many people completed the task?

211
00:05:41,240 --> 00:05:43,180
What percentage dropped off at checkout?

212
00:05:43,180 --> 00:05:44,540
How long did it take?

213
00:05:44,540 --> 00:05:46,760
It tells you what is happening and how much

214
00:05:46,760 --> 00:05:47,610
of it.

215
00:05:47,610 --> 00:05:49,820
Qualitative UX research is about

216
00:05:49,820 --> 00:05:52,080
understanding. Why did the users drop off?

217
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:53,260
What were they thinking?

218
00:05:53,260 --> 00:05:54,640
What confused them?

219
00:05:54,640 --> 00:05:56,580
It's usually smaller and more in depth.

220
00:05:56,580 --> 00:05:57,930
It tells you the why.

221
00:05:57,930 --> 00:05:59,440
Usually, you want both.

222
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:01,660
You want the numbers to show where the problem

223
00:06:01,660 --> 00:06:04,300
is, and you want the conversation to show why

224
00:06:04,300 --> 00:06:05,540
the problem is happening.

225
00:06:05,540 --> 00:06:07,920
Now, the third big way to split UX research

226
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:10,450
is behavioural versus attitudinal.

227
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:11,240
Attitudinal

228
00:06:11,240 --> 00:06:13,450
UX research is about what users say,

229
00:06:13,450 --> 00:06:14,460
what they think,

230
00:06:14,460 --> 00:06:15,559
what they feel,

231
00:06:15,559 --> 00:06:17,619
what they tell you that they do.

232
00:06:17,619 --> 00:06:20,520
Surveys and interviews are mostly attitudinal.

233
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:21,860
behavioural UX research,

234
00:06:21,860 --> 00:06:22,580
on the other hand,

235
00:06:22,580 --> 00:06:24,640
is about what people actually do.

236
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:26,119
So you watch them use the thing.

237
00:06:26,119 --> 00:06:28,280
You look at the data and what they click.

238
00:06:28,280 --> 00:06:29,580
And the reason

239
00:06:29,580 --> 00:06:30,840
why I want to hammer on this,

240
00:06:30,840 --> 00:06:32,360
because this is one of the things

241
00:06:32,360 --> 00:06:34,240
that was so hammered into my ears during my

242
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:35,360
first semester,

243
00:06:35,360 --> 00:06:38,580
that what people say and what people do are

244
00:06:38,580 --> 00:06:39,710
often very different.

245
00:06:39,710 --> 00:06:40,380
What people say,

246
00:06:40,380 --> 00:06:41,920
what they give you in a survey,

247
00:06:41,920 --> 00:06:43,960
is usually different from what they do when

248
00:06:43,960 --> 00:06:45,119
you watch them.

249
00:06:45,119 --> 00:06:46,740
If you ask

250
00:06:46,740 --> 00:06:47,660
someone how they use an app,

251
00:06:47,660 --> 00:06:48,700
they will give you an answer.

252
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:50,100
If you watch them use the app,

253
00:06:50,100 --> 00:06:51,840
they could end up doing something completely

254
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:53,499
different from the answer that they gave you.

255
00:06:53,499 --> 00:06:55,220
And they might genuinely believe what they

256
00:06:55,220 --> 00:06:55,540
told you.

257
00:06:55,540 --> 00:06:56,160
We are human beings.

258
00:06:56,160 --> 00:06:57,980
We are just not very good at noticing our

259
00:06:57,980 --> 00:06:59,260
own behaviours.

260
00:06:59,260 --> 00:07:02,448
So good UX research watches as much as it

261
00:07:02,448 --> 00:07:05,059
asks. What people say still matters.

262
00:07:05,059 --> 00:07:07,080
Sometimes they do exactly what they tell

263
00:07:07,080 --> 00:07:08,940
you. But when what they say and what they

264
00:07:08,940 --> 00:07:09,640
do don't match,

265
00:07:09,640 --> 00:07:12,200
then working out why they don't match is also

266
00:07:12,200 --> 00:07:13,550
a very useful finding.

267
00:07:13,550 --> 00:07:15,440
Now, there are a few more categories of UX

268
00:07:15,440 --> 00:07:16,053
research

269
00:07:16,053 --> 00:07:17,653
that I'll go over very quickly.

270
00:07:17,653 --> 00:07:19,033
The primary research

271
00:07:19,033 --> 00:07:20,253
is the research you do yourself,

272
00:07:20,253 --> 00:07:21,913
like running your own interview.

273
00:07:21,913 --> 00:07:24,153
Secondary research uses what already exists,

274
00:07:24,153 --> 00:07:24,703
like

275
00:07:24,703 --> 00:07:25,653
published studies,

276
00:07:25,653 --> 00:07:27,053
industry reports and all of that.

277
00:07:27,053 --> 00:07:29,273
Remote UX research happens over a video call

278
00:07:29,273 --> 00:07:30,673
through an online tool.

279
00:07:30,673 --> 00:07:32,573
In-person research happens in the same room

280
00:07:32,573 --> 00:07:34,073
where you can notice things like body language

281
00:07:34,073 --> 00:07:35,113
and all of that.

282
00:07:35,113 --> 00:07:36,873
And then we have moderated research,

283
00:07:36,873 --> 00:07:38,493
where a researcher is there,

284
00:07:38,493 --> 00:07:39,973
guiding the session,

285
00:07:39,973 --> 00:07:41,393
asking follow-up questions.

286
00:07:41,393 --> 00:07:42,813
And unmoderated research,

287
00:07:42,813 --> 00:07:45,093
where people complete tasks on their own,

288
00:07:45,093 --> 00:07:47,213
usually through a tool that records them.

289
00:07:47,213 --> 00:07:48,833
So how is UX research actually done?

290
00:07:48,833 --> 00:07:50,683
There are a lot of methods that I've

291
00:07:50,683 --> 00:07:52,352
actually mentioned in the earlier part.

292
00:07:52,352 --> 00:07:53,313
There's user interview,

293
00:07:53,313 --> 00:07:54,273
where you sit down with people,

294
00:07:54,273 --> 00:07:55,652
you talk to them about their lives,

295
00:07:55,652 --> 00:07:55,933
their work,

296
00:07:55,933 --> 00:07:56,753
their frustration.

297
00:07:56,753 --> 00:07:58,053
Maybe how they use a product and all of

298
00:07:58,053 --> 00:07:59,433
that. There's usability testing.

299
00:07:59,433 --> 00:08:01,473
You give someone a task to do with your

300
00:08:01,473 --> 00:08:02,652
design. Maybe with your app,

301
00:08:02,652 --> 00:08:04,213
you watch how they do the tasks,

302
00:08:04,213 --> 00:08:06,122
usually while they think out loud.

303
00:08:06,122 --> 00:08:06,973
Then we have surveys.

304
00:08:06,973 --> 00:08:08,513
You can reach a lot of people quickly with

305
00:08:08,513 --> 00:08:11,213
surveys. But surveys usually have less depth.

306
00:08:11,213 --> 00:08:12,693
And then there's contextual

307
00:08:12,693 --> 00:08:14,412
inquiry, where you go to where people work and

308
00:08:14,412 --> 00:08:16,482
live and watch them in their real environment.

309
00:08:16,482 --> 00:08:18,013
There are also diary studies,

310
00:08:18,013 --> 00:08:20,993
where people record their experiences over

311
00:08:20,993 --> 00:08:21,753
days or weeks.

312
00:08:21,753 --> 00:08:24,073
So you see patterns that maybe a single

313
00:08:24,073 --> 00:08:25,693
session would have missed.

314
00:08:25,693 --> 00:08:27,232
There's card sorting.

315
00:08:27,232 --> 00:08:28,753
There's A/B testing.

316
00:08:28,753 --> 00:08:30,533
There's all kinds of UX research methods that

317
00:08:30,533 --> 00:08:32,223
we can't go over in this video.

318
00:08:32,223 --> 00:08:34,053
You might be thinking that UX research sounds

319
00:08:34,053 --> 00:08:35,833
like something that only big companies with

320
00:08:35,833 --> 00:08:37,223
big budgets can do.

321
00:08:37,223 --> 00:08:39,112
But even as a small startup,

322
00:08:39,112 --> 00:08:41,293
some research is always better than no

323
00:08:41,293 --> 00:08:41,882
research.

324
00:08:41,882 --> 00:08:42,673
You don't need a lab.

325
00:08:42,673 --> 00:08:44,173
Talk to five people who would actually use

326
00:08:44,173 --> 00:08:46,553
your product before you start to build

327
00:08:46,553 --> 00:08:49,332
maybe yourself and a few friends.

328
00:08:49,332 --> 00:08:51,193
Watch three of your friends try your app on

329
00:08:51,193 --> 00:08:52,423
a video call

330
00:08:52,423 --> 00:08:53,092
with messages,

331
00:08:53,092 --> 00:08:53,773
with feedback.

332
00:08:53,773 --> 00:08:54,893
That also counts,

333
00:08:54,893 --> 00:08:57,212
because good design goes so much deeper than

334
00:08:57,212 --> 00:08:58,443
just a nice visual.

335
00:08:58,443 --> 00:09:00,393
It's about whether the thing actually works

336
00:09:00,393 --> 00:09:01,939
for the people using it.

337
00:09:01,939 --> 00:09:04,380
Now, another question I wanted to answer is

338
00:09:04,380 --> 00:09:06,859
that is UX research the same as user research?

339
00:09:06,859 --> 00:09:07,439
Most of the time,

340
00:09:07,439 --> 00:09:08,199
people use these two terms.

341
00:09:08,199 --> 00:09:09,599
They mean the same thing.

342
00:09:09,599 --> 00:09:12,099
You will see even in job titles that use

343
00:09:12,099 --> 00:09:12,889
either one.

344
00:09:12,889 --> 00:09:15,099
Now, user research is the broader one.

345
00:09:15,099 --> 00:09:17,460
UX research sits under it.

346
00:09:17,460 --> 00:09:18,799
User research is about

347
00:09:18,799 --> 00:09:19,919
understanding the users themselves,

348
00:09:19,919 --> 00:09:20,287
the users,

349
00:09:20,287 --> 00:09:20,652
their needs,

350
00:09:20,652 --> 00:09:21,019
their habits,

351
00:09:21,019 --> 00:09:21,799
their lives.

352
00:09:21,799 --> 00:09:23,820
And it can feed into a lot of decisions

353
00:09:23,820 --> 00:09:25,219
that go beyond design.

354
00:09:25,219 --> 00:09:26,019
For example,

355
00:09:26,019 --> 00:09:28,179
research into how customers feel about your

356
00:09:28,179 --> 00:09:30,719
prices or your delivery time is user research.

357
00:09:30,719 --> 00:09:33,139
It isn't really about the experience of your

358
00:09:33,139 --> 00:09:33,940
product.

359
00:09:33,940 --> 00:09:36,379
UX research is more focused on how people

360
00:09:36,379 --> 00:09:38,429
experience a specific product or service.

361
00:09:38,429 --> 00:09:39,034
Either way,

362
00:09:39,034 --> 00:09:40,299
they share the same goal,

363
00:09:40,299 --> 00:09:42,599
which is making sure that decisions are based

364
00:09:42,599 --> 00:09:43,599
on real users.

365
00:09:43,599 --> 00:09:43,899
And again,

366
00:09:43,899 --> 00:09:45,519
both of them also differ from

367
00:09:45,519 --> 00:09:46,119
market research.

368
00:09:46,119 --> 00:09:46,979
For example,

369
00:09:46,979 --> 00:09:48,479
market research is more about what people

370
00:09:48,479 --> 00:09:49,355
would buy.

371
00:09:49,355 --> 00:09:51,415
UX research is about how people use the

372
00:09:51,415 --> 00:09:53,435
product, how it makes them feel,

373
00:09:53,435 --> 00:09:55,165
how it feels to them as well.

374
00:09:55,165 --> 00:09:57,175
Will AI replace UX research?

375
00:09:57,175 --> 00:09:58,535
Very good question that I also wanted to

376
00:09:58,535 --> 00:10:00,535
answer. AI tools are already doing a lot of

377
00:10:00,535 --> 00:10:01,656
the slow work.

378
00:10:01,656 --> 00:10:02,735
They can transcribe interviews.

379
00:10:02,735 --> 00:10:04,526
They can summarise hours of notes.

380
00:10:04,526 --> 00:10:07,045
It saves UX researchers a lot of time.

381
00:10:07,045 --> 00:10:09,235
I cannot even imagine having to do my thesis

382
00:10:09,235 --> 00:10:10,315
four years ago.

383
00:10:10,315 --> 00:10:11,235
I probably would do it,

384
00:10:11,235 --> 00:10:11,656
but oh my God,

385
00:10:11,656 --> 00:10:12,995
it would be a lot of work.

386
00:10:12,995 --> 00:10:14,981
So AI is changing how UX research gets done,

387
00:10:14,981 --> 00:10:15,935
in a good way,

388
00:10:15,935 --> 00:10:16,235
I think,

389
00:10:16,235 --> 00:10:17,276
if you use it well.

390
00:10:17,276 --> 00:10:19,911
Now, if you're thinking about a career in UX

391
00:10:19,911 --> 00:10:21,615
research, I'm still a student.

392
00:10:21,615 --> 00:10:23,175
I'm currently doing my master's in human

393
00:10:23,175 --> 00:10:23,795
computer

394
00:10:23,795 --> 00:10:25,475
interaction. And from what I've noticed,

395
00:10:25,475 --> 00:10:27,675
the number of jobs with the title UX

396
00:10:27,675 --> 00:10:29,245
researcher is actually small

397
00:10:29,245 --> 00:10:31,095
compared to other design roles.

398
00:10:31,095 --> 00:10:32,755
I think it's because a lot of companies expect

399
00:10:32,755 --> 00:10:34,196
their designers and maybe their product

400
00:10:34,196 --> 00:10:36,035
managers to just do some research work

401
00:10:36,035 --> 00:10:36,655
themselves.

402
00:10:36,655 --> 00:10:39,196
So companies have fewer people whose whole job

403
00:10:39,196 --> 00:10:40,155
is research,

404
00:10:40,155 --> 00:10:41,515
but it's still a very much needed skill.

405
00:10:41,515 --> 00:10:43,576
I think it's just the entry level that might

406
00:10:43,576 --> 00:10:44,985
be hard to break into.

407
00:10:44,985 --> 00:10:45,895
I wouldn't know yet,

408
00:10:45,895 --> 00:10:48,316
honestly. I'm not in the job market for UX

409
00:10:48,316 --> 00:10:49,295
research yet,

410
00:10:49,295 --> 00:10:50,845
but that much I do know now.

411
00:10:50,845 --> 00:10:52,956
And as far as to get into UX research,

412
00:10:52,956 --> 00:10:54,715
the way I'm getting into it is by getting

413
00:10:54,715 --> 00:10:55,805
a master's degree,

414
00:10:55,805 --> 00:10:56,956
but I want to believe you can get into

415
00:10:56,956 --> 00:10:57,896
it

416
00:10:58,295 --> 00:10:59,125
different ways.

417
00:10:59,125 --> 00:11:00,155
You can learn the basics,

418
00:11:00,155 --> 00:11:00,675
the method,

419
00:11:00,675 --> 00:11:01,682
when to use one way,

420
00:11:01,682 --> 00:11:02,885
how to ask questions.

421
00:11:02,885 --> 00:11:04,902
There are free guides from places like Nielsen

422
00:11:04,902 --> 00:11:06,522
Norman Group,

423
00:11:06,562 --> 00:11:08,203
Maze, Interaction Design Foundation.

424
00:11:08,203 --> 00:11:09,386
You want a book,

425
00:11:09,386 --> 00:11:09,466
you know,

426
00:11:09,466 --> 00:11:10,917
there are a lot of books as well.

427
00:11:10,917 --> 00:11:13,236
And also try to do real research,

428
00:11:13,236 --> 00:11:14,356
something that nobody's paying you for.

429
00:11:14,356 --> 00:11:15,396
Just pick an app you use,

430
00:11:15,396 --> 00:11:16,636
interview a few people,

431
00:11:16,636 --> 00:11:17,476
watch them use it,

432
00:11:17,476 --> 00:11:18,356
write what you find,

433
00:11:18,356 --> 00:11:19,976
write your first case study or something like

434
00:11:19,976 --> 00:11:21,557
that. You can also volunteer to do research

435
00:11:21,557 --> 00:11:22,756
with small business,

436
00:11:22,756 --> 00:11:23,136
you know,

437
00:11:23,136 --> 00:11:24,116
things like that.

438
00:11:24,116 --> 00:11:26,677
A lot of UX researchers from what I've noticed

439
00:11:26,677 --> 00:11:27,896
come from other fields,

440
00:11:27,896 --> 00:11:29,096
psychology, teaching,

441
00:11:29,096 --> 00:11:30,297
marketing, journalism,

442
00:11:30,297 --> 00:11:30,917
computer science.

443
00:11:30,917 --> 00:11:33,037
So if your job already involves talking to

444
00:11:33,037 --> 00:11:34,616
people, figuring out what they need,

445
00:11:34,616 --> 00:11:35,876
maybe this is something you might love.

446
00:11:35,876 --> 00:11:36,927
And of course,

447
00:11:36,927 --> 00:11:38,576
degrees going to school,

448
00:11:38,576 --> 00:11:39,956
like the way I'm doing,

449
00:11:39,956 --> 00:11:42,186
is actually also a very valid route,

450
00:11:42,186 --> 00:11:43,976
maybe even the most common one.

451
00:11:43,976 --> 00:11:45,866
So that's UX research.

452
00:11:45,866 --> 00:11:46,677
It's a very long video,

453
00:11:46,677 --> 00:11:48,504
so if you got bored in the middle and

454
00:11:48,504 --> 00:11:50,037
skipped through a lot of it.

455
00:11:50,037 --> 00:11:51,396
Just don't forget this phrase.

456
00:11:51,396 --> 00:11:53,337
Good design doesn't start with screens.

457
00:11:53,337 --> 00:11:55,910
Good design starts with people.
